The Farey structure: 1/(k+1) and 2/(2k+1) ARE neighbors (det=1) at every k => the window is a
Farey GAP; the coarsest fraction inside is the mediant 3/(3k+2), q=3k+2. A gap member needs
M=c/q with q>=3k+2 AND realizable by an integer family (q<=2max, opus S109 lever).

STRUCTURE x WIDTH: the n=7 gap member has M=5/33 (q=33). Check q vs 3k+2:
  k=6: M=5/33, q=33; 3k+2=20; q/(3k+2)=1.65; 2*max=34; q<=2max? True

WHY IT SHRINKS: window width ~ 1/(2k^2). A gap member's M sits at a Farey fraction c/q inside,
q>=3k+2, so its 'reach' above 1/(k+1) is quantized in steps ~1/((k+1)q) <= width only if q large.
But q<=2max and single-cluster max~O(k) => q=O(k), while landing in a width-1/(2k^2) window at the
right ALIGNMENT (leave-one-out, S111) becomes infeasible as k grows: the # of q-values in the
window is ~width*q_range ~ (1/2k^2)*(k) = 1/(2k) -> 0. THIS is the n-specific death (structure
alone is n-blind; the width tolerance is the decisive metric factor). Matches mac-mini S17 walls.
